---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getFonts } from '../lib/fonts';
import { MOODS } from '../data/moods';
import { USE_CASE_LABELS, USE_CASES } from '../data/use-cases';

const count = (await getFonts()).length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why Fontlog exists, how fonts get in, and what the notes mean."
>
  <div class="prose max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About Fontlog</h1>

    <p>
      For years I set every project in Bricolage Grotesque. Not because I'd compared it against
      anything &mdash; it just looked right, and picking a font is the kind of small decision that
      quietly eats an evening. Every few months I'd go looking for something new, bounce between a
      dozen tabs of specimens, licenses I couldn't parse, and install instructions that assumed I
      was buying a desktop license, and give up.
    </p>

    <p>
      Fontlog is the thing I wanted instead: a short, opinionated list of open-license typefaces
      I've actually used or seriously evaluated. Not a directory of everything &mdash; Google Fonts
      already does that. Every entry here is one I'd ship.
    </p>

    <h2>How a font gets in</h2>
    <p>
      I've used it on a real project, or I've sat with it long enough to have a genuine opinion.
      That's the whole bar. If it's here, there's a reason, and the note on its page says what it is
      &mdash; including what to watch out for.
    </p>

    <h2>What's on each page</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A live preview you can type your own text into.</li>
      <li>The license in one plain-English sentence, with a link to the real thing.</li>
      <li>
        Copy-paste install snippets: npm / Fontsource, <code>next/font</code>, Google Fonts, and a
        self-host template.
      </li>
      <li>Real sites using the font, when I've found good ones.</li>
      <li>
        A &ldquo;best for&rdquo; list &mdash; the kinds of site the font suits, with a reason for
        each.
      </li>
      <li>A few &ldquo;if you like this, try&rdquo; pointers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The tags</h2>
    <p>
      Every font has a classification (sans, serif, mono, display) and one or more moods &mdash;
      {MOODS.slice(0, -1).join(', ')}, or {MOODS.at(-1)}. They're deliberately vague, because
      &ldquo;I want something warmer&rdquo; is usually how the search actually starts. Use the
      filters on the home page to narrow things down.
    </p>

    <p>
      Separately, most fonts carry a <strong>&ldquo;best for&rdquo;</strong> list &mdash;
      {USE_CASES.map((u) => USE_CASE_LABELS[u]).join(', ')} &mdash; describing the job the typeface does
      rather than how it feels. Every one of those tags has to come with a reason on the font's page;
      a claim I can't justify in a sentence doesn't get made. They're judgement calls, not rules: plenty
      of good typefaces work far outside the boxes I've put them in.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything is open</h2>
    <p>
      Every typeface listed is free for commercial use under an open license (mostly the SIL Open
      Font License). No paid fonts, no affiliate links. The catalogue is small on purpose &mdash;
      {count} right now &mdash; and grows only when I find something worth adding.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
